Retinal Detachment
Opthalmology & ENT
Sudden flashes/floaters, Curtain of vision loss, Undulating gray retina

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-040
Reason for Visit: Acute, painless loss of peripheral vision described as a dark shadow descending over the eye, accompanied by flashing lights.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a textbook presentation of an acute retinal detachment (sudden flashes, floaters, and a peripheral curtain-like visual field defect). This is an absolute, time-critical ophthalmological emergency. Urgent home assessment was mandatory to confirm the diagnosis, restrict patient movement to prevent macular detachment, and facilitate immediate Code 1 emergency transfer to a tertiary eye hospital to salvage the patient's vision.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I saw a massive burst of flashing lights, and now it looks like a thick black curtain has been pulled halfway down over my right eye."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a sudden onset of numerous large, dark floaters (a distinctive shower of black spots) occurring 4 hours ago. This was immediately followed by intense photopsia (spontaneous flashes of light prominently in the peripheral visual field). Over the last 2 hours, the patient developed a painless, rapidly progressive visual field defect classically described as a dark curtain or shadow drawn across the superior visual field. Central visual acuity is currently subjectively intact, but the patient feels the shadow is creeping closer to the center. Denies trauma, eye pain, or redness. Highly myopic (short-sighted).
Functional Status: Immobile, sitting rigidly in a chair, extremely fearful of moving.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; requires immediate emergency transport.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Highly anxious, protecting the right eye, strictly minimizing head movement.
Physical Exam: Visual acuity in the right eye remains 6/9 (macula is currently "on"). Confrontation visual fields reveal a dense, absolute scotoma (loss of vision) in the superior peripheral field of the right eye, directly corresponding to an inferior anatomical detachment. Pupils are equal and reactive; a mild relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD) is noted in the right eye. Direct ophthalmoscopy reveals undulating, gray, opaque retinal folds clearly visible elevating into the vitreous cavity in the inferior fundus. Loss of the normal healthy red reflex in the affected quadrant. Cornea is clear; anterior chamber is deep. Left eye is completely normal.
Environmental Observations: Patient is packed and ready for immediate transport.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Rhegmatogenous Retinal Detachment (Macula-On) - Status: Absolute Surgical Emergency
Differentials:

Posterior Vitreous Detachment (Uncomplicated) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a dense, progressive visual field defect (the "curtain") and the direct visualization of undulating grey retinal folds; simple PVDs cause floaters and flashes but no structural visual field loss.

Amaurosis Fugax (TIA) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The vision loss being progressive and persistent, rather than transient (resolving in minutes), and the presence of prominent flashes/floaters which indicate mechanical traction on the retina, not ischemia.

Vitreous Hemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Direct visualization of the detached, folded retina, whereas a dense vitreous hemorrhage obscures all fundal details completely with a uniform red/black haze.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Impending permanent central blindness)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with the classic, terrifying triad of an acute retinal detachment: flashes, floaters, and a peripheral curtain-like shadow. Immediate face-to-face assessment was critical to confirm the diagnosis and, most importantly, to determine if the macula (central vision) was still attached. The physical exam confirmed an inferior, macula-on detachment. Because the central vision is currently spared, this represents a hyper-acute surgical window; any further physical movement or delay could cause the detachment to tear through the macula, resulting in irreversible, permanent central blindness. Immediate immobilization, postural restriction, and Code 1 ambulance transfer were strictly mandated.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Imposed absolute strict bed rest pending ambulance arrival. Applied a rigid eye shield to the right eye to prevent inadvertent rubbing or pressure. Arranged immediate Code 1 Ambulance transfer to the tertiary ophthalmology center for emergent surgical repair (vitrectomy/scleral buckle).
Positive Behavioral Change: To absolutely minimize intraocular fluid shifts that could further detach the macula, the patient must lie strictly flat on their back and lock their gaze directly on a single, fixed point on the ceiling, refusing to track any movement in the room. This voluntary paralysis of the extraocular muscles actively prevents mechanical sloshing of the vitreous gel against the retinal tear, immediately and directly halting the progression of the detachment while awaiting transport.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Rigid eye shield applied.
Follow-up: Handed over directly to tertiary ophthalmology surgical team.

